  "investigation_notes": {
    "large_gnn_score_explanation": "Near-zero calibration variance on inactive actuators (e.g. F_PU3, S_PU3, F_PU5) causes mu_sq_h = mean(E_cal) + 1e-8 to be approximately 1e-8. When actuators activate or experience minor residuals (e.g. E = 0.004), dividing by 1e-8 yields Z-scores exceeding 300,000, inflating the mean score S to 10,000-125,000. Masking these dominant sensors in fidelity/SHAP naturally eliminates hundreds of thousands in score.",
    "negative_fidelity_explanation": "In residual forecasting S = mean((Y_hat(X) - Y_obs)^2 / mu_sq), if an attack abruptly corrupts the future observed target Y_obs while history X was normal, masking X cannot reconcile the forecast with the corrupted observation Y_obs. Shifting X to normal baseline can slightly increase forecasting error against Y_obs, yielding a genuine negative score drop. This reflects the fundamental difference between predictive anomaly detection and supervised classification.",
    "low_gnn_agreement_explanation": "KernelSHAP evaluates exponential coalition game theory over the graph neural network, whereas LIME fits a local linear hyperplane over Gaussian perturbations in 43D space. In the presence of extreme variance disparities across sensor channels (1e-8 vs 1e1), linear regression slopes diverge from Shapley values, resulting in low top-5 set overlap."
  }
